### Changed
- **Copy-mode installs (`JHOL_LINK=0`) are ~37x faster: 3874 ms -> ~105 ms for a 50 MB tree.** The copy
  path was doing a per-file `fs::copy` (thousands of syscalls). It now tries a **whole-tree**
  materialization first, reusing the existing CAS linker, which picks the best primitive this filesystem
  offers - `clonefile` (macOS/APFS), `FICLONE` reflink (Linux), hardlink, then a byte copy - and falls back
  to the per-file walk (which itself now uses reflink) only when the result would not be a real directory
  of real files. Copy mode still produces real files, never links into the store, so editing a package
  cannot mutate the cache. Link mode (the default) is unaffected.
### Fixed
- **A dependency built for another OS/CPU could be installed anyway.** A **required** package whose
  `os`/`cpu`/`libc` excludes this machine (e.g. `@esbuild/linux-x64` on macOS/arm64) was skipped by the
  resolver with a warning and then materialized regardless, leaving an unusable package on disk. The
  install list is now gated once, at the point where it is consumed, so no route into it (expansion,
  fallback, or lockfile reuse) can put a foreign-platform package on disk. Optional and
  peer dependencies for other platforms were already skipped silently, and the lockfile still records
