### Security
- **A crafted or corrupt JAGR-S manifest could read out of bounds.** The manifest's
  `capacity` field is file-controlled and is used as `mask = capacity - 1` and as the length
  of a slice built with `from_raw_parts`. With `capacity = 0` the mask wrapped in release
  builds and the probe indexed a *zero-length* slice — an out-of-bounds read — while debug
  builds panicked on the subtraction. A sparse file could also claim ~100 GB and pass the
  length check, turning every probe into a multi-billion-entry scan. The value is now
  validated at open: it must be a power of two in `1..=16M`.
- **A server-controlled `Content-Length` was used directly to pre-allocate a buffer.** A lying
  header (a proxy, a mirror, or a compromised registry) made jhol reserve that much memory
  before a single body byte arrived; release builds abort on allocation failure. Pre-allocation
  is now clamped to 32 MiB — the body still grows as the real bytes arrive, so nothing changes
  for honest responses.
- **The daemon acted on requests that never carried the protocol magic.** The struct is read as
  raw bytes and the magic was written but never checked, so any local client could drive an
  install with arbitrary bytes. The daemon now rejects a bad magic with a clear error, and the
  request's `pkg_count` (which arrives off the wire and could be `u32::MAX`) is clamped before it
  is used as a pre-allocation instead of reserving ~96 GB.

### Fixed
- **The install daemon stalled for 30 seconds before every real install.** It handled
  connections one at a time, so a single client that connected and then sent nothing held it
  for the full read timeout. The next request's first socket write blocked for ~30 s (measured
  30.0005 s) even though the install itself takes ~200 ms, and the same stall appeared in
  `jhol install --daemon`, `ci` and every other request. Each connection now gets its own
  thread, and a process-wide lock serializes only the working-directory section (which is what
  made the sequential loop necessary in the first place). Measured: daemon cold install
  **30,206 ms → 239 ms**, a request arriving while an idle peer is connected
  **~30,000 ms → 9 ms**, with concurrent installs still serialized and correct.

### Fixed
- **A dependency range was flattened to a fixed version in the lockfile.** The root
  `dependencies` block recorded the *resolved* version, so `"ms": "^2.1.3"` was written as
  `"2.1.3"` — a later install could never move within the range again. It now records what the
  project declares, so ranges stay ranges and npm-style ranges round-trip.
- **`npm:` aliases, `file:`, `workspace:` and git dependencies were missing from the lockfile's
  root block.** Because they have no registry entry to look up, they were dropped entirely, so the
  lockfile no longer described the project. All of them are now recorded with the specifier you
  wrote (`"my-lodash": "npm:lodash@4.17.21"`, `"local-lib": "file:../lib"`,
  `"git-dep": "git+file://…"`), while an optional dependency the resolver deliberately skipped is
  still left out.
- **`install --offline` failed on a project with an `npm:` alias.** The alias *name* was treated as
  a registry package (`my-lodash@4.17.21`), which exists in no registry and is not in the cache, so
  the offline install aborted even though the alias was already materialized. Alias entries are now
  marked as materialized, like `file:` and git ones.
- **`jhol ci` and `install --frozen` failed on projects with local, workspace or git dependencies.**
  Strict mode demanded an integrity hash for every package, but a linked directory or a checkout has
  none (npm records none either), and an optional dependency that cannot be resolved made the check
  fail outright. Materialized dependencies and skipped optional ones are now exempt, so a
  reproducible project built from local/workspace/git deps passes `ci`.

### Fixed
- **A `git:` dependency was replaced by the registry package of the same name.** For
  `{"rimraf":"github:isaacs/rimraf#v3.0.2"}` jhol installed `rimraf@6.1.3` from the registry
  instead of the checkout (its declared `glob ^13.0.3` is not even published, so the tree was
  also incomplete), overwriting the checkout that had just been materialized. The registry root
  set is now built without re-adding dependencies the spec-grammar pass already materialized,
  so git dependencies resolve to the checkout, their own `dependencies` are installed, and the
  noisy `resolution failed with both resolvers` warning for a supported spec is gone.
  Measured before/after on the same fixture with a fresh cache and project each run: 4 of 6
  runs wrong before, 6 of 6 correct after.
- **The lockfile no longer drops a git dependency's specifier.** It recorded the resolved
  version in the root `dependencies` block (`"git-lib": "4.5.6"`) and the clone URL as
