### Security
- **Cache import rejects payloads that escape the export directory or are symlinks.** The import read a
  `file` field from the manifest and did `dir.join(file)`, then copied that path into the local store — so a
  manifest naming `../../../../etc/passwd` (or an absolute path) would copy that file in, and a symlinked
  payload was followed for the same effect. Payload names must now be plain filenames inside the export
  directory, and symlinked payloads are refused; both fail with a clear message. Verified: traversal and
  symlink manifests are rejected (exit 1, nothing written to the store) while a legitimate unsigned export
  still imports (exit 0, `Imported 1 package`). Real exports name payloads `<sanitised-spec>.tgz`, so this
  costs nothing.

### Security
- **Capsule accessors no longer read out of bounds or produce invalid `&str`.** Three accessors used
  `unsafe { str::from_utf8_unchecked }` (undefined behaviour when the bytes are not valid UTF-8, e.g. an
  offset landing inside a multi-byte sequence), and `entry_deps` sliced the dependency table with **no
  bound check at all** (`from_raw_parts(dep_edges.add(start), count)`). Because the capsule is mmap'd, a
  file that changes after `open()` validated it (TOCTOU) reaches those accessors with out-of-range
  offsets: the old code panicked with `range start index 4294967295 out of range for slice of length 68`,
  and release builds use `panic = "abort"`, so that is a process abort. Strings are now read with a bounds
  check plus real UTF-8 validation, and the dep slice is range-checked; both return empty rather than
  reading out of bounds. Covered by `crates/jhol-core/tests/capsule_adversarial.rs` (crate-local CSV fixture test) (tampered, truncated, corrupt-UTF-8, and
  post-open-mutation capsules): red before the fix, green after.

### Removed
- **The unused `simd-json` dependency and the empty `simd` feature.** `simd-json` was declared but
  referenced nowhere, and `simd = []` gated nothing (no `cfg(feature = "simd")` existed) — yet it was in
  `default`, pulling 12 crates into every build (lockfile packages 496 -> 484). Measured before removing:
  parsing the largest packument (8.69 MB) takes ~31 ms even in Python, so the whole metadata set is worth
  ~0.5% of an install. Build weight removed, no capability lost.

### Added
- **Every install now records an entry in the rolling install log** (`install-log.jsonl`, pruned to the
  last 6 hours). The module existed, compiled and had passing tests, but nothing ever called it, so the
  data did not exist at all. Each entry carries the project directory, the installed `name@version`
  list, elapsed time, cache-hit/network counts and success — the data a "recent installs" view needs.

### Fixed
- **Lifecycle scripts actually run now.** The executor (`lifecycle.rs`) existed, compiled and
  had its security gate, but was **never declared as a module**, so nothing could call it:
  `--scripts`, `--script-allowlist` and `trustedDependencies` silently ran nothing. Verified
  before the fix (a package with a `postinstall` did not execute it even with the allowlist
  set) and after, in all four combinations: `--no-scripts` -> not run; `--scripts` without an
  allowlist -> **not run** (the allowlist stays the gate); `JHOL_SCRIPT_ALLOWLIST=<pkg>` ->
  runs; `trustedDependencies: ["<pkg>"]` in package.json -> runs. Scripts run at most once per
  process, on materialization only, never on a no-op or fast path.

### Changed
- **Installing from a warm cache is 1.2-2.4x faster on Apple Silicon** (measured: the
  ~300-package fixture drops from 186.6 ms to 76.6 ms; the 20-dep fixture from 148.6 ms to
  121.4 ms, medians of 5 with correctness asserted). Writing the lockfile needed the SRI
  integrity of every package and re-hashed all tarballs on every run; a digest of a
  content-addressed tarball never changes, so it is now memoised next to it
  (`store/<hash>.tgz.sri`). The lockfile tail on the ~300-package fixture went from **296 ms
  to 12 ms**. Integrity output is unchanged (51/51 entries) and `jhol ci` still verifies
  against that lockfile.
- **The lockfile tail honours the configured registry.** It built `https://registry.npmjs.org/...`
  by hand when a package had no resolved URL, which sent private package names to the public
  registry; it now uses the shared registry-aware URL builder.
- **`http-reqwest` is no longer a default feature** (opt in with `--features http-reqwest`).
  The `http2_download` module measured 1.45x slower than the ureq path on packuments with
  parity at best on tarballs; its verified win is resumable downloads only. The default
  build no longer carries reqwest and its transitive HTTP/2 stack for a module nothing on
  the hot path calls. `tokio` stays a mandatory dependency (`cas` uses it unconditionally).
  The client also no longer forces `http2_prior_knowledge()`, letting ALPN negotiate H2 —
  the likely cause of the packument gap; re-measure before any wider use.

### Added
- **`scripts/bench/scales.py` — a reproducible seven-scale benchmark** (1, 2, 5, 20 and
  ~300 dependencies cold, plus warm-cache and unchanged-project re-installs) with the protocol
  this project settled on: a fresh cache *and* a fresh `HOME` per cold run, a discarded
  pre-warm before warm runs, medians with min/max, and every run asserted to produce a usable
  tree. Results are stored under `benchmarks/macos-arm64/`.

### Fixed
- **`jhol install <pkg>` now installs the package it was asked for, alongside your existing
  dependencies (last P0).** Two compounding faults: the flat expansion used only the requested
  packages as roots (so every dependency from `package.json` looked dropped and the nested-layout
  fallback always triggered), and that fallback resolves the manifest alone (so the request itself
  was discarded — the command reported success having installed everything *except* what was asked
  for). Expansion roots are now the union of the request and the manifest, and the requested
  packages (with their transitive tree) are merged into the nested resolution as well. Verified
  on a project with 20 dependencies *and* a genuine conflict: `jhol install is-number@7.0.0`
  installs `is-number` and keeps every existing package; a regression check was added to the QA
  matrix, which now runs **59 checks**.

### Fixed
- **A deleted package directory is repaired again (P0).** Three fast paths decided
  "already up-to-date" from a fingerprint that records only the manifest hash
  (`lockfile_fingerprint_matches`, `try_capsule_install`'s warm bypass, and the
  pre-argument-parsing shortcut), so removing `node_modules/<pkg>` was silently accepted and
  never restored. The status record now stores the **`node_modules` entry count** (reusing the
  first 8 bytes of its existing padding, so the file format stays 125 bytes) and every one of
  those gates requires it. Verified: deleting a package directory, changing the manifest,
  wiping `node_modules`, and adding a dependency all behave correctly, and an unchanged
  re-install is **7.5 ms** (Bun: 7.8 ms).

### Fixed
- **jhol's own warm-start machinery works again — the "node_modules is already fresh"
  check had never been able to succeed.** Two writers produced the fingerprint in
  incompatible formats: `daemon_cache::write_nm_fingerprint` writes the 8 raw hash bytes
  that `nm_is_fresh` compares, while `install_package`'s marker writer wrote **hex text**,
  so the comparison could never match. The installer now uses the single existing writer,
  which turns the native fast paths on: an unchanged re-install drops from **126 ms to
  ~10 ms** with no new cache or marker mechanism, and invalidation keeps working (manifest
  change, `add`, a deleted package directory, and a removed `node_modules` all take the
  full path).
- **The pre-argument-parsing shortcut no longer skips a command that names packages.**
  `try_exit_before_clap` only inspected `argv[1]`, so now that the fingerprint check works,
  `jhol install <pkg>` could have exited 0 on a fresh `node_modules` and silently ignored
  the request. The shortcut is skipped whenever a non-flag operand follows the subcommand.

### Changed
- **An unchanged re-install is ~16x faster: 126 ms -> 8 ms** (Bun: 7.8 ms). Re-running
  `jhol install` in a project where nothing changed used to walk the manifest, the
  lockfile, `special_deps` (with its workspace BFS), rewrite the capsule, scan all of
  `node_modules`, relink the store links and rewrite markers — about 120 ms to learn that
  nothing had changed. A marker recording the manifest hash, lockfile hash and
  `node_modules` entry count now short-circuits that tail, so a changed dependency, a
  changed resolution, or a removed/added directory all still take the full path
  (all four invalidation cases verified).
### Fixed
- **The capsule fast path no longer answers an explicit package request.** `try_capsule_install`
  replayed the previous resolution for `jhol install <pkg>`, reporting success without
  installing the named package. It is now skipped when packages were named on the command line.

### Fixed
- **`JHOL_LINK=0` is honoured everywhere now.** The documented "copy from the store
  instead of linking" mode was silently ignored for any package installed through the
  selective extractor: its `link_or_copy_tree` always created a directory symlink and
  only fell back to copying if the symlink call itself failed. It now checks the link
  mode first and copies when linking is disabled. Verified: with `JHOL_LINK=0`
  `node_modules/<pkg>` is a real directory and still loads; by default it remains a
  symlink.

