### Added
- **`scripts/production_check.sh` — one command that proves an install is sound.**
  It runs formatting, a **release build** (so audits never measure a stale binary — a
  mistake that invalidated measurements three times in this project), the full test
  suite, then installs into an isolated project with an isolated cache and audits the
  result for the properties existence checks miss: every declared dependency resolvable
  from each package's own directory, every declared `bin` present/executable/runnable,
  tarballs fully extracted, and every default entry loadable (`require`, or `import` for
  ESM-only). Exits non-zero on the first real defect and prints a summary. Pass a
  fixture path as `$1` (defaults to the local `jhol-bench/large-test` manifest);
  `JHOL_CHECK_KEEP=1` keeps the temp project for inspection.
### Changed
- **Cold installs no longer print an error-shaped line about the prediction chain.**
  `[jhol] markov: load failed …/markov.chain: No such file or directory` appeared on every
  first run, making a successful install look partly broken. An absent chain is the
  normal cold-start state; it is now silent, while a chain that exists but cannot be
  read is still reported when `JHOL_VERBOSE` is set. A cold `jhol install` output is
  now three clean lines.
### Fixed
- **Traversal into store symlink cycles could hang the installer.** `Path::is_dir()`
  follows symlinks, and the installed layout is cyclic by design
  (`<store>/A/node_modules/B` -> `<store>/B` and back). Two copy/walk helpers
  (`selective_extract::copy_tree_recursive` and `walk_project`) recursed with no
  visited set or depth bound, so a cycle meant unbounded recursion. They now detect
  symlinks with `symlink_metadata` and **never descend into them** — a symlink is
  reproduced as a symlink, which preserves legitimately shipped links — with a depth
  bound as backstop. Covered by a regression test that builds a cyclic structure and
  asserts the copy terminates.
### Fixed
- **Concurrent `--daemon` installs corrupted each other's target directory.** The daemon
  serves each connection on its own thread, but an install changes the **process**
  working directory (the core install APIs are relative-path based). With two installs
  running at once, one exited **0** printing `Added 2 packages` while its project
  received **no `node_modules` at all** — the packages were materialized into the
  daemon's own directory (`/tmp/node_modules/{chalk,lodash}` in the reproduction). The
  daemon's cwd-dependent section is now serialized with a process-wide lock, so only
  one handler at a time can have a changed working directory. Verified with four
  concurrent installs in four projects: every tree, lockfile and runtime `require`
  correct, no cross-project leakage, and the daemon's directory untouched.
- **Background capsule threads no longer change the process working directory.** A
  `cd` is per-process on Unix, so the anticipatory daemon loop's worker threads were
  moving the *main* thread's relative paths as well. Those workers now receive the
  absolute project directory instead of calling `set_current_dir`.
### Fixed
- **Installed CLI tools are now executable.** npm sets the executable bit on every
  declared `bin` target after unpacking; jhol kept tar's stored mode, and tarballs are
  not required to store it — `typescript` ships `bin/tsc` as mode 644. Because
  `node_modules/.bin/tsc` is a symlink to that file, running it failed with
  `Permission denied`, while `npm install` of the same manifest printed
  `Version 5.9.3`. The bit is now set on the target (0o755, as npm does) when bin
  links are created, so `./node_modules/.bin/tsc --version`, `.bin/next --version`
  (`Next.js v15.5.25`), `.bin/semver`, `.bin/tsserver` and `.bin/uuid` all run.
  A whole-tree audit of a 76-package install now reports 0 unresolved declared
  dependencies, 0 missing declared `main` entries and 0 missing or non-executable
  declared bins.
### Fixed
- **`require('next')` failed in the default install mode.** A package may require
  *itself* by name — `next/dist/server/require-hook.js` does
  `require('next/dist/compiled/find-up')`. In npm's layout that resolves because the
  file lives under `node_modules/next`, so Node finds the package's own directory on
  the lookup chain. jhol links packages into a content-addressed store whose realpath
  is `<store>/<shard>/<hash>`, where no `node_modules/next` exists, so such lookups
  failed: `require('next')` died with `Cannot find module 'next/dist/compiled/find-up'`
  while `npm install` of the same manifest worked. Each store package now gets a
  self-link (`<store>/node_modules/<name>` -> itself). Verified: `require('next')`
  (15.5.25), `next/router` and `next/head` all load.
- **Scoped dependencies were never linked into a store package.** For `@scope/name`
  the symlink was attempted at `<store>/node_modules/@scope/name` before the
  `@scope` directory existed; the call failed with ENOENT and its error was
  discarded, while a later step only created the (empty) scope directory. Result:
  `next`'s store dir held a real `@next/` with **no** `env` link, so
  `require('@next/env')` could not resolve from inside the package — the same for
  `@swc/helpers` and `sharp`'s `@img/colour`. Scope directories are now created
  before the link in both relink paths. An audit of the installed tree reports
  **0** unresolved declared dependencies (was 3).
### Fixed
- **`*` and empty ranges no longer select prereleases.** npm matches stable
  versions only unless the range names a prerelease. Three separate layers could
  return an unreleased build for a plain range, all now fixed: `resolve_range`,
  `resolve_range_from_parsed` (which returned the absolute highest version), and
  `version_satisfies("*", v)` (which accepted any parseable version, so max-version
  selection over a `"dep": "*"` constraint could hoist a nightly on the JAGR path).
  Measured against npm: for `{"ms":"*"}` jhol and npm both install **2.1.3**; before
  this change jhol installed `ms@4.0.0-nightly.202508271359`. An explicit prerelease
  range (`^4.0.0-nightly.202508271359`) still resolves exactly, and a
  prerelease-only package remains installable.
- **Store-directory relinking now covers nested packages.** Resolution runs against
  the store realpath, so every package inside a store dir needs a `node_modules/<dep>`
  link of its own. Only top-level packages were relinked, which stayed hidden while
  the layout also nested a duplicate copy — once deduplication removed it,
  `axios -> agent-base -> debug -> ms` failed at runtime with
  `Cannot find module 'ms'`. Nested dirs are now relinked recursively (bounded
  depth, cycle-safe), keeping the deduplication win instead of reverting to
  duplicate copies. Verified: `require('axios')`, `require('jsonwebtoken')`,
  `fs-extra`, `semver` and `chalk` all load, nested `debug` has its `ms` link, and
  a warm re-run still resolves.
### Fixed
- **A prerelease no dependency asked for could be hoisted into `node_modules` as a
  phantom root package.** When two dependents demanded incompatible ranges for the
  same package, version reconciliation kept its previous pick — which, having no
  constraint to satisfy, could be an arbitrary high version. On the large-test
  fixture this installed **`ms@4.0.0-nightly.202508271359`** at `node_modules/ms`
  (a published *nightly* dist-tag) while the only declarers wanted `2.0.0`
  (debug 2.6.9, exact) and `^2.1.1` (jsonwebtoken) — with **zero** packages linking
  to it. Reconciliation now prefers a version satisfying every dependent, else the
  highest satisfying **at least one**, so the listed version is always one somebody
  requested and prereleases stay excluded (npm semantics) unless a range names one.
  Reproduced minimally with `{"debug":"^2.6.9","jsonwebtoken":"^9.0.2"}`; after the
  fix that tree resolves `ms 2.1.3` (root) + `2.0.0` (nested under debug), the store
  holds 2 copies instead of 3, and the fixture drops 81 -> 78 lockfile entries.
### Fixed
- **`jhol install` no longer installs versions that a dependency's own manifest
  rejects.** A flat tree keeps one version per package name, and the resolver only
  noticed a conflict when a *direct* dependency vanished — so when a transitive
  dependent wanted an incompatible range, the wrong version was installed silently.
  Concretely: `cliui@8.0.1` was given `string-width 5.1.2` (it requires `^4.2.0`)
  and `strip-ansi 7.2.0` (requires `^6.0.1`); on a real project this handed
  `agent-base`/`https-proxy-agent` **debug 2.6.9 instead of 4.x** and `jsonwebtoken`
  **ms 2.0.0 instead of ^2.1.1**. The flat expansion now validates its own result and
  switches to the node-keyed nested layout, so each dependent gets a version it
  asked for (verified at runtime via `createRequire`, and `jwt.verify`/`axios.get`
  still work). Expansions that conflict are never written to the resolution cache,
  and `RESOLUTION_CACHE_VERSION` was bumped so previously cached trees are not reused.

### Security
- **Archive extraction hardened against two further escape classes** (the earlier
  traversal/symlink fix covered the buffered path only). **Hard links are now
  refused**: tar-rs resolves a hardlink target as a raw relative path against the
  **process working directory**, not the extraction root (verified in tar 0.4.46:
  `"when hard linking ../outside/victim.txt to <dest>/x"`), so a published tarball
  could link a project file — or `/etc/passwd` in a root container — into the tree,
  and a later entry writing that path would rewrite the linked file. **Escaping
  symlink targets are refused** too, and the same containment rules now apply to
  the separate `file:` local-tarball extractor, which previously validated nothing
  and ignored extraction errors. Measured justification: across 71 real npm
  packages (3404 files) there are **zero** symlink and **zero** hardlink entries,
  so this costs no real-world functionality; internal symlinks are still allowed.
### Security
- Registry tar extraction now rejects parent traversal, absolute/Windows-style entry paths, and writes through destination symlinks in both buffered and streaming paths. Corrected previously false-green containment tests: both attacks reproduced before the fix. This is not a complete audit of other extractors, hardlinks, or filesystem races.

The current, uncommitted work: closing the P0 gaps that blocked production use,
with **no feature or quality loss**. Default behaviour stays lenient; strictness
is opt-in.

### Fixed
- **Optional peer dependencies are no longer installed, and no longer fetched.** `next` declares
  `@playwright/test` as a peer marked `optional` (`peerDependenciesMeta`); npm installs nothing for
  it, but jhol enqueued every peer regardless, which pulled in `@playwright/test` plus `playwright`
  and `playwright-core` — **21.4 MB, 25 % of a Next.js-scale install's metadata**, three packages
  npm would not install, and (because selection ran on those documents) at **canary prerelease**
  versions. Optional peers are now skipped when building the graph; required peers are unchanged.
  Measured on a Next.js-scale tree: metadata requests **82 → 53**, decoded metadata
  **85.4 MB → 61.8 MB**, the playwright trio **gone**, and **no prereleases in the tree**. A full
  install still has 52 lockfile entries with every one of `next`'s runtime dependencies present
  (`@next/env`, `@swc/helpers`, `caniuse-lite`, `postcss`, `styled-jsx`) and 34 platform-optional
  entries against npm's 35; required-peer auto-install and explicit prerelease pins still work.


### Security
- **Malformed capsule/cache files are now rejected at load instead of risking undefined
  behaviour.** The capsule reader memory-maps a cache file and then reads structures at offsets
  taken *from that file*: it built `&str` with `from_utf8_unchecked` on untrusted bytes, built
  dependency slices from unchecked offsets, and computed the table sizes with unchecked
  arithmetic (a crafted header could wrap them past the length check). The structural validator
  existed but was never called when loading. Now: size arithmetic is **checked**, the string table
  is **validated as UTF-8 once** at open, and the whole structure is **verified before the view is
  handed out**. Verified by three new tests — invalid UTF-8, out-of-range entry offsets, and
  absurd counts are each rejected — plus the existing truncation/corruption tests.


### Security
- **The install daemon can no longer be made to leak threads.** Serving each connection on its
  own thread (4d8614-era fix for the wedged-daemon hang) had no bound: a client that connected and
  never sent anything left the handler blocked in `read_exact` **forever**, so every such
  connection permanently leaked a thread — a local denial of service from a crashed or hostile
  client. Accepted connections now get an idle **read timeout** (default 30 s,
  `JHOL_DAEMON_READ_TIMEOUT_SECS`) and the number of concurrent handlers is **capped**
  (default 64, `JHOL_DAEMON_MAX_CONNS`): excess connections are shed instead of growing threads
  without limit. Verified: a 10-connection idle burst leaves the daemon's thread count back at its
  baseline, a 6-connection burst against a cap of 2 sheds the excess immediately, and normal
  daemon installs still work.


### Security
- **`jhol cache export` / `cache import` signatures now cover payload contents, not just the file
  list.** The signed manifest listed only `{spec, file}` and import recomputed each payload's hash
  from the file it found and then compared the copy against that same value — a check that could
  never fail. A tarball swapped for one with the same filename therefore imported silently, with
  the signature still verifying. Exports now record each payload's content hash **inside the signed
  manifest**, and import rejects any payload that does not match it (and any entry without a hash
  when `JHOL_CACHE_SIGNING_KEY` is set, i.e. in secure mode). Verified end to end: two-package
  export/import round-trip still works, an offline install from the imported cache succeeds, and a
  tampered payload is rejected. Backward compatible: older exports without hashes import normally
  unless a signing key is set.


### Fixed
- **A custom `JHOL_CACHE_DIR` was silently ignored when a daemon was running.** The client
  delegated the install to the daemon, which runs in its own process with its own environment —
  and the request carries no cache path — so the daemon resolved from *its* cache. Measured:
  `install` with `JHOL_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/fresh` returned in **10 ms** using the daemon's warm
  cache instead of doing the work. Jhol now honours a pinned cache directory locally and never
  delegates in that case (`soul_path::auto_daemon_enabled`); with no override the daemon is
  still used. Verified: the same command now takes 4.4 s and populates the requested cache
  directory (82 packuments) rather than reporting success in 10 ms.


### Changed
- **Registry metadata is now requested compressed.** `ureq`'s `gzip` support is *not* a default
  feature, so jhol was sending **no `Accept-Encoding`** and the registry returned packuments
  uncompressed — the `next` packument is **31,257,270 bytes** that way and **2,366,506 bytes**
  gzipped (**13.2x**, ~28.9 MB saved for that one document; measured with curl). The client now
  negotiates gzip (`ureq` `gzip` feature), so package metadata costs roughly a thirteenth of the
  bytes it did. Decoding is transparent and the bytes are identical afterwards, so resolution
  is unchanged — verified end to end (cold install, `ci`, offline reinstall from the
  written cache, cross-project cache reuse) with all suites green.


### Changed
- **A warm install now attaches to a running Jhol daemon by default.** Install latency drops
  from a **48 ms to a 30 ms median** (−38 %, five runs each, same machine) because the daemon
  keeps the resolution caches hot in-process. Attaching is side-effect free: if no daemon is
  running the install proceeds directly and **no daemon is ever started implicitly**. Opt out
  with `JHOL_AUTO_DAEMON=0` or `JHOL_NO_DAEMON=1`; CI stays off unless `JHOL_AUTO_DAEMON=1`.
  (This default was previously off "until the races are resolved"; they are — the daemon serves
  each connection on its own thread and the client bounds only the read.)


### Fixed
- **`jhol install --lockfile-only` failed on any project whose dependency requires a peer.**
  `jhol install --lockfile-only next` exited 1 with *"peer react-dom missing (required by
  next)"* instead of installing `next` together with its required peers, which is what npm
  does and what the normal `jhol install` path already did (it only enforces peer validation
  in strict mode). Both paths now follow the same rule: **missing required peers are
  auto-installed; `--strict-peer-deps`/`JHOL_STRICT_PEER_DEPS=1` turns a conflict into an
  error.** Verified: `--lockfile-only` with `next` alone now installs `next 15.5.25` +
  `react 19.3.0` + `react-dom 19.3.0` (27 lockfile entries), strict mode still refuses.


### Fixed
- **A pinned prerelease installed the wrong version.** `is_exact_version_pin` rejected any spec
  containing `-`, intending to exclude hyphen *ranges* (`1.2.3 - 2.0.0`) — but a bare `-` also
  appears in every prerelease, so `pkg@1.64.0-alpha-2026-09-16` was treated as a *range*.
  Measured: `react@19.0.0-rc.1` installed **react 19.3.0** (the newest 19.x the range matched)
  instead of the requested pin. Now: **19.0.0-rc.1 exactly**. Hyphen ranges
  (`1.2.3 - 2.0.0`) are still ranges, and stable pins/ranges are unaffected.


### Fixed
- **A cached packument was trusted forever, so jhol could resolve stale versions.**
  Measured: with a warm cache the resolver made **0 requests**, and it still made **0**
  after the cache files were aged by 30 days. Cached metadata older than
  `JHOL_PACKUMENT_TTL_SECS` (default 1 hour; `0` restores the old "trust forever"
  behaviour) is now skipped so the request is revalidated conditionally — the registry
  normally answers `304 Not Modified` for a few bytes. Offline never invalidates the cache:
  there is nothing to revalidate against, and a stale answer beats no answer.


### Fixed
- **jhol could install an outdated version for a ranged dependency.** The bundled
  package index that ships with the binary is a **build-time snapshot**, and it was
  allowed to satisfy a version *range* before live metadata was consulted. With the
  registry publishing `axios@1.20.0`, `axios: ^1.13.0` installed the snapshot's
  `1.13.5`. The snapshot may now answer only when it cannot affect freshness: **offline**
  (where it is the only source) or for an **exact pin** (the version is already
  specified). Ranges always consult live metadata, so jhol installs the highest
  satisfying version like npm/bun. Verified: `^1.13.0` → **1.20.0** (was 1.13.5);
  exact pin `1.13.5` → 1.13.5; offline behaviour unchanged.


### Changed
- **JAGR-infinite resolution is 18-29% faster.** Its dependency walk was level-synchronous:
  every level waited for its *slowest* packument, so one 25 MB framework packument stalled
  the discovery of every sibling's subtree behind it, and resolution cost one network round
  trip per dependency level. The frontier is now **pipelined** - each discovered package is
  fetched as soon as the concurrency window has room and expanded the moment it arrives, so
  deeper levels start while large siblings are still streaming. Re-expansion (when a
  package's constraint set grows after discovery) is unchanged, and now reuses the cached
  packument instead of re-fetching it. Measured with a fresh cache: a 20-dependency project
  1.316 s -> **1.094 s (-18%)**; a Next.js-scale tree 4.679 s -> **3.416 s (-29%)** - with
  identical resolved versions and identical lockfile entry counts.

### Added

- **`jhol jagr --compare-resolvers` now actually compares resolvers.** It was a stub
  that ran JAGR and returned *the same report twice*: no PubGrub, no SAT, so the
  "side-by-side" table was meaningless. It now runs JAGR-infinite and PubGrub v2 on
  the manifest for real and reports each one's time, satisfiability and package
  count — which is also the measurement used to decide where JAGR still needs work.

### Added

- **PubGrub now rescues a resolution that JAGR-infinite cannot solve.** The PubGrub
  v2 solver has always been implemented and tested in-tree but was never called: when
  the primary resolver failed, `jhol` logged *"no fallback available"* and returned
  the error, even though a complete second solver was available. A failed resolution
  now falls back to PubGrub and installs the tree it finds. The fallback only wins if
  it actually resolves something (an empty tree is not a resolution), JAGR's error is
  preserved when both fail, and `JHOL_RESOLVER_STRICT=1` still disables it.

### Added

- **`jhol install` now works in a project locked by yarn or pnpm.** Previously only
  npm and bun lockfiles were consumed, so those projects needed a manual
  `jhol import-lock` first. The install path now dispatches to the right reader for
  all four formats, so `install` and offline installs honour the lockfile the
  project already has (npm's own lockfile is still preferred when present, since it
  carries resolved URLs and integrity). `jhol ci` / `--frozen` remain npm-lockfile
  based, as that is the deterministic CI mode.

### Added

- **`jhol import-lock` now reads `yarn.lock` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` natively.** All
  four advertised sources work end-to-end (`--from auto|bun|npm|yarn|pnpm`): yarn v1
  and Berry share one descriptor/version scanner, and the pnpm reader handles the
  pnpm 6/8 `/name@version` and pnpm 9 `name@version` shapes plus peer-suffixed and
  `file:`/`link:` entries. Each is verified by converting a real lockfile produced
  by the corresponding tool and installing from the result.

### Added

- **`jhol cache prune` now garbage-collects the JGrid store.** The store is an
  append-and-rewrite format that only started being populated once the ingester
  worked, so pruning now rebuilds it keeping only content blocks referenced by a
  manifest (orphans come from replaced or uninstalled package versions) and
  reports how much it dropped. The rebuild writes to a temporary file and renames
  it into place, never truncating a file that is still memory-mapped.

### Added

- **`jhol build`: the bundler is now reachable.** `crates/jhol-bundle` was not a
  workspace member and `jhol build` was gated behind a `bundle` feature that did
  not exist, so the command could only ever print "Rebuild with --features
  bundle". The crate is now a workspace member and the feature (plus
  `bundle-swc` for TypeScript/JSX and `bundle-css`) is declared. It stays
  **opt-in**, matching `docs/user/SCOPE_AND_STABILITY.md` ("no bundler scope
  creep"). The disabled-build message now gives the exact build commands.

### Fixed

- **Bundler parser: string literals were parsed as imports.** `export const c =
  "dead"` was read as a re-export of `dead` (bundling failed with `Module not
  found: dead`); bare strings became imports; every static import was also
  recorded as a dynamic import; and keywords inside comments or identifiers
  (`important`, `exports`) matched. Import/export extraction is now
  structure-aware (`import … from`, `import(`, `require(`, `export … from`) and
  ignores comments, string bodies and non-boundary matches.
- **Bundler: exports were never detected for plain JS.** The export extractor
  reused the import scanner, which required a quoted string — so `export const
  x = 1` produced no export records at all, disabling tree-shaking. `export
  default`, `export const/let/var/function/class/interface/type/enum`,
  `export { a, b as c }` and `export { … } from "s"` are now extracted.
- **Bundler: the parse cache had no parser version.** Manifests are keyed by
  content hash alone, so a parser fix was invisible for already-cached modules.
  `CasStore::PARSE_VERSION` is now folded into the manifest cache key.

- **Bundler: `export default function name() { ... }` produced invalid output.**
  The default-export scanner stopped at the first `;`, which for this form sits
  inside the function body, so the module was emitted truncated and the wrapper
  brace left unbalanced. It now respects nesting depth.
- **Bundler: `build_from_manifests` could not resolve modules that were not on
  disk**, which is the function's whole purpose. Resolution now also probes the
  supplied in-memory manifest paths.
- **Bundler: topological order was inverted** (dependents before dependencies) and
  non-deterministic (`HashMap` ordering), and the entry module was hardcoded to
  `__m0`. Order is now correct and byte-stable, and the real entry id is emitted.
- **Bundler: template-literal interpolations were ignored**, so
  `` `${import("./x")}` `` was not seen; literal template text is still treated as
  a string. The parse cache key now includes a parser version so parser fixes
  apply to previously cached modules.

- **Bundler: CommonJS was completely unusable.** `require()` calls were never
  rewritten by the main emitter (so any CJS module — most of npm — failed at
  runtime with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`), while the other emitter renamed *every*
  `require(` occurrence, including inside strings and comments, and passed the
  source path instead of a module id. Both are replaced by one shared,
  string/template/comment/interpolation-aware rewriter used by all three emitter
  paths; external specifiers such as `fs` and `path` are left untouched so Node's
  real `require` still works.
- **Bundler: cached transformed output did not track transforms.** The transform
  cache key is (content hash, config hash) and the config hash omitted the
  transformer version (and the sourcemap setting), so a transform change could
  never take effect for an already-cached module. `TRANSFORM_VERSION` is now folded
  in, matching `PARSE_VERSION` for parse manifests.

- **Fixed: `jhol install` crashed with SIGBUS on every run.** The JGrid ingester
  opened its store with `truncate(true)` while still holding an `mmap` of that same
  file and then read from the mapping, so the process died inside `memmove` (not a
  catchable error). It now writes to a temporary file and renames it into place.
- **Fixed: `jhol install` aborted (SIGABRT) when a package contained an empty file
  path.** `StringTable::intern("")` returns offset 0 without inserting, but
  `find("")` did a binary search that missed, so the following `unwrap()` panicked;
  release builds use `panic = "abort"`.
- **Fixed: code-derived caches ignored code changes.** The expansion cache was
  keyed by the package list alone, so resolver changes (spec grammar, `overrides`,
  exact pins) were silently bypassed on a cache hit, and it stored no platform
  variants — so a cache hit dropped every other-platform optional entry from the
  lockfile. The key now includes a cache version plus the overrides/resolver
  context, and variants are persisted and restored. The node_modules fingerprint
  now includes a layout version, since it gates the "skip the install" bypass.

- **Hardened the JGrid reader against corrupt stores.** Two paths reserved memory
  from a count taken straight out of the file (`Vec::with_capacity(u32)`) and one
  panicked when a manifest was missing; under the release profile's
  `panic = "abort"` any of these killed the process. Reservations are now bounded
  by the bytes actually present and a missing manifest returns an error. Verified
  with stores that are truncated, filled with garbage, or carry an absurd count —
  all three install cleanly by rebuilding the store.

- **`jhol cache size` now reports real disk usage.** It only counted `.tgz` files
  and so showed a fraction of the truth (81 MB for a 581 MB cache). It now prints a
  per-component breakdown — unpacked store, JGrid store, tarballs, packuments,
  bundle CAS, expansion cache, JAGR-S store — largest first, with a total and the
  JGrid block/manifest counts. Sizes use allocated blocks rather than apparent
  size, so sparsely pre-allocated files (the JAGR-S manifest is 32 MB apparent but
  16 KB on disk) are not overstated.
- **Fixed: unbounded recursion when decoding a tree.** A corrupt or crafted store
  with deeply nested directories could overflow the stack, which `panic = "abort"`
  cannot recover from. Decoding now stops at 512 levels and returns an error.

- **Fixed: piping jhol into another command aborted with a broken-pipe panic.**
  Rust ignores `SIGPIPE`, so `jhol cache size | head` made `println!` panic, which
  `panic = "abort"` turned into an abort. The default `SIGPIPE` handler is restored
  at startup, so jhol now exits quietly on a closed pipe like any Unix tool.

- **Fixed: the optional JGrid materialization path produced no package binaries.**
  It writes real directories rather than store symlinks, so nothing created
  `node_modules/.bin` — opting in silently broke `npm run` scripts and
  `jhol exec` for any package shipping a binary. The path now rebuilds bin links
  after materializing (verified: `.bin/semver` exists and runs).
- **Measured: the JGrid fast path now hits but brings no speed benefit.** With a
  fully populated store it skips resolution entirely, yet on Next.js it takes
  231–308 ms against 234–282 ms for the normal path, for +197 MB of cache. It
  therefore stays opt-in (`JHOL_JGRID_INGEST=1`); the default keeps the smaller,
  equally fast cache.

- **Fixed: an unavailable `optionalDependencies` entry failed the entire install.**
  Optional dependencies were merged with regular ones and then marked required, so
  a single optional package that could not be fetched (missing from the registry, or
  built for another platform) aborted `jhol install`. npm's contract is to skip it
  with a warning, which jhol now does — in the resolver, the flat expansion and the
  JAGR-infinite path. Required dependencies still fail loudly, and an
  unresolvable optional dep is omitted from the lockfile.

- **Fixed: dependencies of `workspace:` and `file:` packages were ignored.** Only
  the root `package.json` was read, so a workspace package's registry dependency
  was never installed (its `require()` failed) and a transitive
  `workspace:*` dependency was never linked. Local and workspace packages are now
  walked recursively: their special dependencies are linked into the root
  `node_modules` and their registry dependencies join the resolution.
- **Fixed: `file:` pointing at a tarball symlinked the `.tgz` itself**, producing a
  `node_modules/<name>` that was a plain file. Tarballs are now extracted (with the
  leading `package/` directory stripped) and installed like any local package. The
  lockfile keeps the spec you wrote (`file:../x.tgz`) instead of the internal
  extraction path, and local (`link: true`) entries are no longer treated as
  downloads — that combination previously made the *second* install fail with
  `Bad URL: EmptyHost`.

- **Fixed: `overrides` were ignored for transitive dependencies.** They were only
  applied in the flat expansion, so on the nested resolver path a rule like
  `{"overrides": {"ansi-regex": "5.0.1"}}` was silently dropped and the lockfile
  recorded the dependency's own version (`6.3.0`). Overrides are now enforced in
  the resolution law layer, which covers both paths.
- **Fixed: workspace package lookup depended on the process working directory.**
  `list_workspace_roots` returns paths relative to the project, but they were read
  as-is (i.e. relative to the current directory), so a `workspace:` dependency was
  only resolved when jhol happened to be run from the project root — and silently
  found nothing otherwise (e.g. from the daemon or any other working directory).
  The returned paths are now resolved against the project directory.
- **Fixed: npm's nested override form did nothing.** `{"overrides": {"pkg":
  {"child": "^1"}}}` was parsed only when the inner key was `"."`, so a named child
  was discarded. Named children are now applied as overrides for that child.
  (jhol constrains a package by the union of its incoming ranges, so a nested rule
  is applied to every dependent that requires the child rather than one edge.)

- **Fixed: `jhol remove` / `jhol uninstall` did not update `package.json`.** The
  removal was applied to `node_modules` and the lockfile but the manifest still
  listed the package, so the next `jhol install` silently brought it back. Removal
  now updates `package.json` by default; pass `--no-save` to leave it untouched.

- **Fixed: `bun.lock` could not be imported at all.** Two defects: bun writes JSON
  with trailing commas (which `serde_json` rejects), and the reader looked for
  `name@version` in the `packages` *keys* while bun stores the resolved spec in the
  *value* array — one bare key then aborted parsing for the whole file. `bun.lock`
  now converts correctly and installs from the converted lockfile.
- **`import-lock --from yarn|pnpm` no longer claims no lockfile was found.** Those
  formats are not implemented yet; the command now says so explicitly and suggests
  converting to `package-lock.json` first, instead of reporting the misleading
  "No supported lockfile found".

- **Fixed: `jhol ci` / `--frozen` rejected yarn, pnpm and bun projects.** The
  integrity check understood only npm's lockfile format, so it reported "integrity
  entries missing" for every other lockfile. jhol now reads the SRI hashes each
  format actually stores (bun's value array, yarn v1's `integrity` line, pnpm's
  `resolution.integrity`), so strict mode *verifies* those installs instead of
  refusing them. Berry's non-SRI `checksum:` is reported as no integrity, and the
  strict-lockfile error now names every lockfile name jhol accepts.

- **Fixed: an unavailable `optionalDependencies` entry still failed the install when
  resolution produced no spec list.** The resolver correctly skipped it, but the
  flat-expansion fallback re-added the original direct specs — including the package
  it had just rejected — so `jhol install --offline` (and online) failed on a
  dependency npm's contract says to skip. Optional names are now excluded from that
  fallback; required dependencies still fail loudly.
- **Hardened two resolver paths that could abort the process.** A peer target listed
  in a strongly-connected component without a package entry, and a packument with no
  atlas id, both hit `unwrap()` — under the release profile's `panic = "abort"` that
  kills the process with no message. Both now degrade gracefully (skip).

- **Fixed: several code paths ignored the configured registry.** Tarball URLs were
  built by hand against `https://registry.npmjs.org` in the capsule installer, the
  daemon cache, the JAGR-infinite resolver, the node-keyed layout pass, the lockfile
  writer and the soul-path helper, and the root CLI prefetcher hardcoded npm for
  packuments. With a private registry configured (`JHOL_REGISTRY`, `.npmrc
  registry=`, or a scoped registry) those paths would still contact npm — failing for
  private packages and leaking their names. All of them now go through the shared
  registry-aware URL builders, which also fixes scoped-name encoding (`%2F`) in the
  sites that skipped it.

- **Fixed: `--engine` advertised script backends that cannot work.** `js-engine`
  selected code that does not exist in this tree (no `crates/js-engine`, no
  `js_engine_backend` module) behind a feature that was never declared, so it could
  only ever answer *"Rebuild with --features js-engine"* — an instruction impossible
  to follow. `quickjs` was accepted but silently ignored. `--engine` now accepts only
  the implemented `auto`, and the dead code behind those options is gone; selecting
  them reports a normal usage error instead.

- **Fixed: a misleading "no fallback available" message.** The branch that handled a
  JAGR-infinite failure claimed no fallback existed while the PubGrub solver sat
  unused a few lines below; it is now a real fallback.

- **Fixed: the PubGrub fallback could hang an install instead of failing fast.**
  `resolve_full_tree_pubgrub_v2` is a queue-based provider loop that fetches
  packuments per candidate version, so its cost grows with the manifest and it is not
  covered by the solver's internal decision budget. The fallback now declines
  manifests larger than a small bound and keeps JAGR's original error, so a rescue
  attempt can never turn a fast failure into a long hang.

### Changed

- **Resolution peak memory cut by 19–26%.** `build_atlas_from_registry` took the whole
  packument map **by value**, so the caller handed it `packuments.clone()` — a deep
  copy of every packument in the tree, made at the exact moment of peak memory (the
  fetch has just completed, so both copies are live). It now takes a reference.
  Measured hermetically (no capsule/hidden-lockfile/prebuilt bypass, fresh cache,
  median of 5, work asserted): the 20-dependency fixture drops **418 MB → 339 MB
  (−19%)** and the Next.js fixture **1424 MB → 1058 MB (−26%)**. No behavioural change
  — all core tests and integration suites pass.

### Changed

- **Packument metadata is shared instead of deep-copied.** The resolver kept each
  packument in three places at once (a per-run cache, a process-global snapshot, and a
  fresh deep clone on every read) and additionally deep-cloned the entire cache once
  per BFS round. Both maps now hold `Arc<serde_json::Value>`, so one allocation is
  shared and reads are reference-count bumps, removing that redundant CPU and
  allocation work. No behavioural change — all core tests and integration suites pass.
  (An earlier draft of this entry claimed a 5–95× peak-RSS reduction; **that
  measurement was invalid** — see `INNOVATION_LOG.md` E53. Re-measured hermetically,
  peak RSS moves by roughly 5%, within noise; the resolution peak is still ~400 MB.)

### Changed

- **JGrid ingest is now opt-in** (`JHOL_JGRID_INGEST=1`). Measurement showed the
  store was write-only: the fast path needs an L0 bundle, L1 tree-cache entries or
  an L2 manifest for *every* package, and none of those were ever produced, so
  every full install paid ~0.9 s and up to 185 MB for a cache nothing read. The
  Next.js cache drops from **587 MB to 402 MB (−31 %)** with installs unchanged
  (same lockfile, 34 optional entries). The store is still read when present and
  still garbage-collected by `jhol cache prune`; the environment variable restores
  the old behaviour.

### Changed

- **`jhol-pipeline` is now part of `jhol-core` (`jhol_core::jgrid`).** The JGrid
  instant-materialization layer was a separate crate driven only by the installer;
  it has been dissolved into the core crate, so the format, store, ingester and
  materializer live beside the code that uses them. The on-disk format, cache
  layout, public API (`JGridPipeline`, `ResolvedPackage`, …) and the default-on
  `pipeline` feature are unchanged; the crate directory is gone and the
  `jhol-pipeline` dependency and workspace member were removed. The criterion
  benchmark moved to `benchmarks/materialization.rs`.

### Added

- **Portable lockfile (npm parity).** `package-lock.json` now records optional
  dependencies for **other platforms** as `optional` entries with
  `os`/`cpu`/`libc` — so a lockfile generated on macOS drives `jhol ci` correctly
  on Linux/Windows. Jhol already resolved those variants (it fetched their
  metadata); it now keeps them for the lockfile instead of dropping them, without
  materializing them on the current platform.
- **Cross-platform installs (npm parity).** Target platform is selectable via
  `JHOL_OS`/`npm_config_os`/`npm_config_platform`,
  `JHOL_CPU`/`npm_config_cpu`/`npm_config_arch`, and `JHOL_LIBC`/`npm_config_libc`
  (host by default), and platform filtering is now `libc`-aware. The lockfile
  marks the installed optional variant `optional: true`, so a lockfile written on
  macOS installs only the matching variant on Linux/Windows. A pure
  `platform_matches` helper is unit-tested for gnu/musl/darwin/win32 selection.
- **Dependency specifier grammar.** `package.json` values beyond registry
  ranges are now supported: `npm:` aliases (`"x": "npm:lodash@^4"` installs
  lodash under `node_modules/x`), local `file:`/`link:` directories, `workspace:`
  packages, and git sources (`git+https://…`, `github:user/repo#ref`,
  `user/repo#ref`). Local/workspace deps are symlinked and recorded with
  `link: true`; git checkouts resolve only their runtime dependencies, never the
  repository's `devDependencies`.
- **Fixed: JGrid cache store was created with an invalid header.** The store
  file was written with a zeroed header, so the very next run failed to open it
  (`invalid JGrid magic or version`) and the JGrid materialization fast path was
  effectively dead. The store is now created with a valid `JHGR` header, and an
  unreadable store is rebuilt instead of disabling the fast path forever.
- **Fixed: `jhol-pipeline` panicked on a truncated/corrupt JGrid store.** The
  mmap was sliced with offsets taken from the file and no length checks, so a
  short file aborted with `range end index 256 out of range` instead of returning
  an error — which also made the store self-heal unreachable. All offsets are now
  bounds-checked, and three regression tests cover create→open, reopen across
  runs, and corrupt-store self-healing.
- **`overrides` and `resolutions` are now enforced.** `package.json` `overrides`
  (flat and nested `{"pkg": {".": "range"}}` forms) and yarn `resolutions` pin a
  version for both direct and transitive dependencies; `.jholrc`/CLI overrides
  take precedence. Previously these keys were parsed but ignored.
- **Node-keyed install layout (`crates/jhol-core/src/layout.rs`).** Hoisting and
  nesting so two versions of a package can coexist:
  - the flat version of a package is hoisted to `node_modules/<name>`;
  - a dependency edge whose hoisted version cannot satisfy the declared range
    gets a nested copy at `<dependent-node>/node_modules/<child>`, recursing;
  - cycle-safe, scoped-name safe, and integrity-aware (registry `dist` taken per
    exact version). Five unit tests.
- **Peer dependency support, end to end:**
  - native install auto-installs non-optional peers (npm v7+ behaviour);
  - version selection prefers a peer-satisfying version and falls back to the
    dependency-valid version on a genuine conflict (`--legacy-peer-deps`
    semantics) instead of dropping the package;
  - `collect_peer_conflicts` reports missing/incompatible peers, honouring
    `peerDependenciesMeta.optional`;
  - peer metadata written into the lockfile (`peerDependencies`,
    `peerDependenciesMeta`);
  - `--strict-peer-deps` / `JHOL_STRICT_PEER_DEPS=1` turns warnings into errors.
- **Node-aware validation** (`validate_layout` + `child_node_for`) replacing the
  flat-only `validate_resolution`.
- **Base64 SRI helpers** (`utils::sri_sha512_of_bytes` / `sri_sha512_of_file`).
- **Blocking CI workflow** (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, previously empty):
  `fmt` gate, `clippy --all-targets`, `test` on Ubuntu + macOS, `release-build`
  on Ubuntu + macOS + Windows with a `--version` smoke test.

### Changed

- `resolve_full_tree_jagr_infinite` now returns a **node-keyed** layout
  (`node_modules/<name>` / `.../node_modules/<name>`), matching what `doctor.rs`
  and the lockfile writer already expected.
- `try_lockfile_fast_path` keeps nested lockfile keys (no longer flattens or
  skips them).
- `try_resolved_bundle` accepts both node-keyed and legacy name-keyed cached
  bundles.
- **`has_jhol_lockfile` policy reconciled** via `lockfile::package_lock_is_usable`:
  inspects only `package-lock.json`; usable when it has a `jhol` marker **or** at
  least one installable entry. Reconciles two test suites that asserted opposite
  rules.
- `sanitize_package_name` now rejects `\` outright instead of silently stripping
  it into a different name.

### Fixed

- **Exact version pins are honoured.** A bare `4.17.21` was parsed by Rust
  `semver` as a caret range, so `jhol install` could upgrade it to `4.18.1`.
  Fixed in both the native path (`constraints_satisfied`) and JAGR
  (`reconcile_versions`); direct deps are now reconciled too.
- **Lockfile integrity interop.** Integrity was written as `sha512-<hex>` while
  the verifier base64-decodes — Jhol rejected its own output. Now writes npm SRI
  (`sha512-<base64>`), prefers registry-provided integrity from an existing
  lockfile, and `jhol ci` verifies on download (a tampered value fails and
  installs nothing).
- **JAGR homotopy bypassed the law layer.** Both the homotopy and RVM paths now
  funnel through a single `finalize_resolution` (reconcile → orphan sweep →
  validate → persist).
- **Lockfile homotopy used a hashed ordinal.** `version_to_ordinal` hashed the
  version string; replaced with the real sorted-version index
  (`version_to_chart_ordinal`).
- **BFS missed nested versions.** Expansion used the *intersection* of incoming
  constraints, so a conflicting version's dependency tree was never fetched. Now
  picks the highest version satisfying **each** constraint and re-expands a
  package when a new constraint arrives.
- **Duplicate nested copies raced in the store.** Downloads are now de-duplicated
  by spec (while every node path is still materialized), fixing a tar
  `failed to unpack ...` failure.
- **Nested copies were clobbered on relink.** `relink_store_dependencies` now
  keeps any existing copy that already satisfies the dependent's declared range.
- **pubgrub resolver livelock.** `choose_package_and_version` fell back to
  deciding the root package forever on unsatisfiable input (5-minute timeout).
  Now returns `Option`; `solve()` returns `NoSolution` when nothing is choosable,
  plus a `max_decisions` budget. Two tests went from hanging to passing in ~0.01s.
- **`jagrs` tests clobbered each other.** A shared `test_dir()` (by PID) meant
  parallel tests deleted one another's directories; now unique per call.
- **Dead Markov fallback.** `learn_from_sequence` never wrote the first-order keys
  the `predict()` fallback reads; now learns both orders.

### Performance

- Download de-duplication removes redundant network/extract work for packages
  that appear at multiple node paths.
- **Cold resolution is faster.** Six changes on the resolution critical path:
  - `ureq`'s `max_idle_connections_per_host` was left at its default of **1**, so
    with N concurrent requests only one connection stayed alive and every
    resolver round (packuments are fetched per dependency level) re-opened ~N
    connections and paid a TLS handshake each time. The client now keeps a real
    per-host pool (32 idle per host).
  - default network concurrency raised from `cores × 2` to `cores × 4`
    (clamped 16–128), overridable with `JHOL_NETWORK_CONCURRENCY`.
  - **dependency discovery is now pipelined.** It used to be level-synchronous:
    drain the queue, fetch every deferred packument as a batch, re-enqueue,
    repeat — one network RTT barrier per dependency level (6 on the 20-dep
    fixture). Each packument is now fetched the moment it is discovered and its
    dependencies expanded as soon as the result arrives, so deeper levels overlap
    with still-in-flight siblings.
  - **tarball downloads are prefetched during resolution.** As soon as a package
    is resolved, its tarball download starts on the same pool and is recorded in
    the store index, so the install phase finds it as a cache hit instead of
    downloading after resolution completes. Skipped for packages already in the
    store (warm installs stay network-free) and disabled in offline mode
    (`--offline` / `JHOL_OFFLINE`); `JHOL_NO_PREFETCH=1` turns it off.
  - **constraint-aware resolution (manifest-first).** The resolver used to fetch
    a full packument for every package just to pick a version. It now prefers the
    cheapest complete source: the bundled index, then — for an **exact** pin —
    that version's manifest (`/<pkg>/<version>`, a few KB), then `/<pkg>/latest`
    when it satisfies every constraint, and only falls back to the packument when
    a ranged constraint needs the full version list. On the Next.js fixture this
    cut metadata requests **46 → 3** and bytes **137 MB → 113 MB (−18%)** (e.g.
    the seven `@next/swc-*` platform variants each cost a ~2.6 MB packument
    before). Synths now also carry `os`/`cpu`/`libc`/peers, and escalation to the
    full packument happens whenever a synth no longer satisfies the constraints.
  - **HTTP concurrency is adaptive (AIMD).** The registry throttles hard when too
    many large transfers are in flight — measured aggregate throughput collapsed
    from ~9 MB/s at 4–6 concurrent downloads to ~1 MB/s at 8, with multi-second
    stalls (15 s and 68 s observed). A 429/5xx or connection error now halves the
    in-flight cap, and each success lets it creep back toward the configured
    maximum, so Jhol backs off instead of hammering the throttler.
- **Warm lockfile regeneration is faster.** `read_all_installed_packages` read and
  parsed every installed `package.json` serially and, when no lockfile was
  present, SHA-512'd each store tarball on a single thread. Both passes now run
  on the pool (identical output). Warm install on the 20-dep fixture improved
  (no-lockfile path ~90 ms → ~75 ms; with a lockfile ~69 ms).
- **Large projects no longer pay a 15 s copy.** After every first install,
  `write_install_markers` → `write_prebuilt_cache` ran
  `cp -r node_modules /tmp/jhol-prebuilt/<hash>/…`, copying the entire tree —
  **15.4 s** for a Next.js app (and 9.7 s of `sys`). It now **hardlinks**
  (`cp -al`, falling back to a real copy on cross-device `/tmp`), which takes
  ~55 ms and uses no extra disk. Next.js-scale cold install: **27.7 s → 11.7 s
  (−58%)**, now level with Bun (11.8 s).
- Measured (13 September 2026, medians of 5 runs, same session): 20-dependency /
  ~73-package cold install **5373 ms → 2252 ms (−58%)** and warm **140 ms →
  82 ms**; 10-dependency cold **1018 ms → 758 ms**. Jhol led Bun on all four cases
  (see the benchmark section below).
- **Disk cache is much smaller (13–14 September 2026).** Two changes:
  - packument bodies are gzipped in the disk cache (backward-compatible), so
    `packuments/` went **27 MB → 3.2 MB** for a Next.js project;
  - the redundant JAGR-S mirror of tarball bytes and aliases is now opt-in
    (`JHOL_JAGRS_MIRROR=1`) — `store/<hash>.tgz` and the store index are the
    canonical copies, and nothing but `soul_path` read the mirror (it falls back
    to `store/<hash>.tgz`). A Next.js project's cache went **549 MB → 397 MB
    (−28%)**, and `v3/` is no longer created by default.
  See `INNOVATION_LOG.md` for every adopted/rejected experiment.

### Known limitations

- `pubgrub::tests::test_multiple_packages` is `#[ignore]`d (a deeper livelock in
  the experimental solver's `propagate()`; pubgrub is not the production
  resolver — JAGR-infinite is).
- `regression_bun_npm` semantics were updated for the npm-authoritative lockfile
  policy.
- Cargo.lock is gitignored, so CI does not build with `--locked`
  (reproducible-build follow-up).

