## [Unreleased] - warm install 2.4x faster: index the store instead of re-walking it

> **Record note (2026-10-03).** This is the **second** `## [Unreleased]` block in
> this file; the first (the current-release section) is near the top at
> `CHANGELOG.md:26`. Both blocks are kept exactly as written — history is not
> merged, reordered or rewritten. The pair is recorded in
> `docs/archive-record/DOC_DUPLICATES.md` (R2, round 4).

### Fixed
- **Warm install was O(entries x store_size).** `install.rs::package_already_resolved`
  walked every `store_unpacked` shard and read + parsed every `package.json` on each
  call (~15k file reads for a 26-package install), so installs got slower the more was
  already cached. The store is now indexed once per cache dir per process
  (`OnceLock<Mutex<HashMap<PathBuf, HashMap<String, Vec<String>>>>>`, keyed by cache
  dir) and each entry is an O(1) hash lookup.

### Performance (macOS arm64, 10 cores, medians of 5 interleaved runs, 26 packages)
- Warm install: 38 ms -> 16 ms (2.4x), faster than Bun 1.3.14 at 23 ms.
- Cold install: 910 ms -> 674 ms (1.35x).
- No functional change: `install::` 18 tests pass, `jhol ci` exit 0, installed tree
  loads under Node.

### Notes
- Bun cold-install comparisons are not stable on a dev machine: Bun also reads a
  global `~/.bun/install/cache` that `BUN_INSTALL_CACHE_DIR` does not redirect
  (762 ms warm-global vs 2194 ms after that cache was cleared). Report medians of
  interleaved runs and pin both caches before publishing a number.

### Investigated (no code change): cold install is round-trip bound
- Measured with `JHOL_HTTP_TIMING=1`: TTFB is 95-98% of every request; a cold
  install is one abbreviated packument per package plus one tarball each, the same
  shape as npm/Bun. Concurrency is not the lever (64x made it slower).
- Retracted an earlier bad reading that the bundled `binary-packages` path cost
  ~1.2 s on cold installs: `JHOL_BINARY_PACKAGES_DIR` pointing at a dir without
  `index.json` is ignored, so both arms of that test used the same path and the
  difference was network noise.
- Bun's cold number on a dev machine swings 762-2194 ms with no code change
  (global `~/.bun/install/cache` state). Interleaved medians put the two tools
  within the noise. Do not publish a cold comparison without pinning both caches.

### Changed: HTTP concurrency controller recovers proportionally
- `ConcurrencyLimit::recover()` stepped `+1` per success while `back_off()` halved the
  window (floor 2). Across a 30-60 request install an additive step cannot recover from a
  halving, so a 429 would leave the window collapsed for the rest of the run. Recovery is
  now proportional (`+ max(1, cur/8)`), and only **429** is treated as a congestion signal
  (a 5xx is retried without permanently lowering throughput). Retry sleep cap 5 s -> 1.5 s.
- Robustness fix, **not** a measured performance win: peak concurrent connections measured
  25 with and without `JHOL_DOWNLOAD_CONCURRENCY=64`, so no collapse reproduced on this
  machine. No before/after timing is claimed.

### Investigated (no change): cold install is tarball-latency bound
- With a lockfile, a cold install fetches **0 packuments** and 30 tarballs totalling
  1.1 MB. Resolution is correctly skipped.
- `JHOL_HTTP_TIMING=1` does not cover the tarball path, so tarball timings are invisible;
  add timing there before further tarball-path work.

### Investigated: cold-install hypothesis ladder (5 rejected)
- Decomposition measured: cold is **89% network** (910 ms), 10% unpack (99 ms), link 19 ms
  for 30 tarballs totalling 1.1 MB.
- Rejected with evidence: packument/download concurrency (64x is slower), the prebuilt
  `binary-packages` index (1.9% coverage of a real tree), AIMD concurrency collapse
  (peak 25 concurrent connections, unchanged by the knob), retry/backoff (no effect, no
  rate-limit evidence), and tarball-path bypassing the pooled agent (it uses
  `get_bytes_with_bearer`, the shared pooled Agent).
- Side finding: when a tarball fetch **fails**, the install still costs ~800 ms because the
  error branch retries with backoff doubling from 250 ms and the install waits for every
  worker. One flaky tarball can add ~750 ms to an install.
- `JHOL_HTTP_TIMING=1` does not instrument the tarball path, which is why this took so
  many experiments. Instrument that path before further work.

### Diagnosed: cold install is per-connection setup cost, amplified by concurrency
- Instrumented `HttpClient::get_bytes_with_bearer` (`[TB-T]` under `JHOL_HTTP_TIMING=1`);
  the tarball path previously emitted no timing at all despite the tracing helper's doc
  comment claiming it covered tarballs.
- Measured: per-request latency is uncorrelated with size (4 KB = 762 ms, 550 KB = 341 ms),
  and mean per-request latency is **47 ms at concurrency 1** vs **396-669 ms at
  concurrency >= 4** - connection reuse works, concurrent new connections are throttled.
  That is why the concurrency curve was flat from 2 to 32: wall-clock gains were cancelled
  by per-request latency growth.
- Fix indicated (not yet implemented): multiplex tarball downloads over one connection
  (HTTP/2) via the existing non-default `http-reqwest` path in `http2_download.rs`.
  Projected cold ~250-350 ms vs ~850 ms. Must be measured - an older "parity on tarballs"
  note contradicts this and predates the measurement.
- Six further hypotheses rejected with evidence (config caching, constant init, store-index
  lock, retries, transfer size, DNS) - all recorded in AGENTS.md.

### Investigated: HTTP/3 unavailable, HTTP/2 measured at 2.2x for tarball downloads
- `registry.npmjs.org` answers `HTTP/2 200` from Cloudflare with **no `alt-svc` header**,
  so HTTP/3 cannot be discovered or used; a QUIC client would have no endpoint. Re-check
  with `curl -sI` if npm ever enables it.
- Reference-client measurement on the same 30 tarballs (medians of 3): h1.1 over 30
  connections 533 ms vs **h2 multiplexed over one connection 245 ms = 2.2x**. This matches
  jhol's own instrumentation (47 ms/request at conc=1 with one reused connection vs
  400-670 ms/request at conc>=4).
- Projected cold install ~850 ms -> ~495 ms (~1.7x overall, 2.2x on the download phase);
  the ~250 ms fixed floor is unaffected by protocol choice.
- Implementation path identified: route tarball downloads through the existing
  `http2_download.rs` behind the non-default `http-reqwest` feature; keep ureq for
  packuments. Not yet implemented or measured.

### Added: HTTP/2 tarball downloads (default), pool tuned
- `http_client::get_bytes_with_bearer` (the tarball path) now routes through the existing
  `http2_download` module; `http-reqwest` is a default feature. `JHOL_NO_HTTP2=1` forces
  the old h1.1/ureq path for A/B comparison; h2 errors fall back to h1.1 automatically.
- `DEFAULT_POOL_SIZE` 4 -> 2, measured (pool=1 596ms | 2 583ms | 4 816ms | 8 828ms true
  cold): fewer connections is better because h2 multiplexes over them.
- Result, true cold with a fresh cache dir per run (28 tarballs, downloads verified):
  **h2 median 536 ms and stable (489-757 ms)** vs **h1.1 median 10878 ms but ranging
  584 ms to 15.3 s**. An independent curl reference on the same URLs reproduces the same
  shape (h1.1 907 ms-67 s, h2 240-288 ms), so it is protocol/network behaviour. The
  durable win is eliminating the 10-15 s tail.
- Warm installs unchanged (6 ms both protocols, 10 interleaved runs). Cost: binary
  6.5 -> 7.5 MB, startup 4 -> 6-7 ms.
- Methodology recorded: wiping `store`/`store_unpacked`/`store_index.json` is NOT a cold
  install (a capsule satisfies it with 0 downloads, 6 ms); a true cold run needs a fresh
  `JHOL_CACHE_DIR`, and every cold timing must verify tarballs were actually downloaded.

### Benchmarked against Bun with both caches pinned (jhol 1.0.2 vs Bun 1.3.14)
- Protocol: fresh `HOME` + fresh cache dir per run for both tools, daemons disabled,
  interleaved rounds, medians with ranges. 5 deps / 26-28 packages, macOS arm64.
- **Cold: jhol 591 ms vs Bun 748 ms - jhol is now 1.27x FASTER** (HTTP/2 tarball
  multiplexing). Supersedes all earlier "cold is slower" readings, which were taken
  against a Bun with a warm global `~/.bun/install/cache`.
- **Warm: jhol 136 ms vs Bun 50 ms - jhol is 2.7x SLOWER.**
- Located the warm bottleneck: `JHOL_PROFILE_INSTALL=1` shows
  `stage=capsule_fast_path delta_ms=129 total_ms=130` - 129 of 130 ms is
  `try_capsule_install`. Ruled out re-extraction (markers/file counts identical before and
  after), network, resolver, and `.bin` work.
- Root cause: the "zero-parse" capsule still reads **every** package's `package.json` from
  `store_unpacked` on each warm install, because `project_package` calls
  `bin_links::link_bins_for_package`, which does `fs::read_to_string(package.json)` just to
  test for a `"bin"` key. ~8 syscalls + 1 scattered read per package; ~100 ms on a cold page
  cache, which is why the same warm install measures 25-33 ms hot and 130-452 ms cold.
- Fix identified (not implemented): carry a `has_bin` bit in the capsule entry so warm
  materialization does zero `package.json` reads.

### Docs: research-paper claims reconciled with measured reality
- Corrected the "faster than Bun on warm installs" claim, which appeared in four papers and does
  not reproduce: measured warm is 136 ms vs Bun 50 ms (**2.7x slower**), no-op 25 ms vs ~7 ms.
  The cold claim survives and is stronger: **jhol is 1.27x faster than Bun cold** (591 ms vs
  748 ms) now that HTTP/2 tarball multiplexing has landed.
- Updated `docs/research/JAGR_INFINITE_RESEARCH_PAPER.md`,
  `docs/engineering/RESEARCH_PAPER_HOLOGRAPHIC_DEPENDENCY_RESOLUTION.md`,
  `docs/engineering/RESEARCH_PAPER_GRM_INSTANT_LOCK.md`, and
  `docs/design/JAGRS_STORE_V3_BREAKTHROUGH_RECORD.md` (whose Synth/Bundled rows are now labelled
  OFFLINE, since they do no network I/O and are not cold installs), each with a measurement
  protocol note. `RESEARCH_PAPER_JAGRS_STORAGE.md` needed no change.
- Flagged the still-unverified claims (JAGR-S v3 microbenchmarks, SSDP syscall/extraction
  figures, 342-package resolution, 6.7 MB RSS, test-suite totals) and noted that the JAGR-S
  microbenchmarks describe a path that ships **disabled** by default.
- `has_bin` capsule flag investigated and rejected: `extract_flags` receives a lockfile entry,
  which never contains `bin`, so the flag cannot be derived without reading package.json anyway
  - and a wrongly-cleared flag would break `node_modules/.bin`.

### Fixed: warm install 5.7x faster (136 ms -> 24 ms) - now beats Bun
- **Root cause:** `soul_path::ensure_jagrs_migrated()` called `jagrs::init_store()` (which
  is `global_store()`) on every install. That initializes the **JAGR-S** store, which the
  code itself documents as opt-in and unread (`jagrs.rs:619-631`, `JHOL_JAGRS_MIRROR=1`).
  Instrumented profile: **140 ms of a 144 ms warm install (97%)**.
- **Fix:** return immediately unless `jagrs::mirror_enabled()`; the gate is checked before
  latching `MIGRATED` so enabling the mirror later still migrates.
- **Result:** warm **136 ms -> 24 ms** (Bun 50 ms) - jhol is now **2.1x faster than Bun
  warm**; no-op 25 -> 20 ms; cold unchanged at 591 ms (1.27x faster than Bun).
  `capsule_fast_path` 143 ms -> 4 ms. This makes the research papers' warm-install claim
  real in both direction and magnitude.
- Supporting: `rebuild_bin_links` is now parallel (was a serial per-package scan), and the
  redundant per-package `link_bins_for_package` calls in `project_package` are removed -
  every `package.json` was being read twice per warm install. `.bin` correctness re-verified.
- Added env-gated stage marks to the capsule warm path under `JHOL_PROFILE_INSTALL=1`.

### Investigated: bundled-offline and no-op claims
- **Offline bundled cold is broken at the system level**: installing 6 packages that ARE in
  `binary-packages/index.json` offline took 3.8-4.8 s and produced 2 directories - offline
  resolution requests network packuments for transitive deps instead of using the local
  archive metadata, and offline misses still pay HTTP retries. Fix scoped: serve transitive
  metadata from `binary-packages/.unpacked/<hash>/package.json`/`deps.json` when offline and
  fail fast without retries.
- **No-op 14 ms vs Bun 11 ms**; `--version` floor is 7 ms and any subcommand dispatches in
  10 ms, so the papers' 5.3 ms no-op is a process-startup claim, not an install claim.
- `jhol --version` 7 ms; splash/quiet knobs make no difference.

### Fixed (partial): offline installs now serve bundled-package metadata locally
- Offline packument misses for packages present in `binary-packages/index.json` are now
  served from the prepacked archive metadata (`index.json` + `deps.json`) instead of failing
  the entire transitive expansion.
- HTTP retries are skipped entirely in offline mode (offline misses previously burned ~4 s of
  backoff per install against an unreachable registry).
- Coverage caveat recorded: the 200-entry bundled index does not contain the transitive
  closure of the popular trees (e.g. @babel helpers), so the papers' 24.6 ms bundled-cold
  figure remains out of reach until the prepacked set is regenerated to include full
  closures. The data is already on disk in `binary-packages/.unpacked/`.

### Measured: cold install at ~190 ms (lockfile-cold, 26 packages) - download phase adds
only ~16 ms over the local unpack floor under HTTP/2 tarball multiplexing; Bun sits at
644-748 ms. The remaining local cost is `store_unpacked` population (~174 ms for 30
packages, ~1464 files, APFS-bound). Local-floor reduction paths identified
(clonefile ingress, deferred store-index flush); full local closure data would make the
bundled-cold claim real.

