## Upgrade notes — production-readiness pass (2026-10-03)

Three corrections from the production-readiness upgrade (details in
`docs/production/PRODUCTION_READINESS.md`).

### Compatibility aliases made honest

`--resolver {pubgrub-v2,pubgrub,jagr,jagr3,jagr-infinite,legacy}` are
compatibility aliases: every accepted value routes to JAGR-Infinite, the only
primary resolver, and a non-default value logs a note. When JAGR-Infinite fails,
a PubGrub-named bounded rescue is attempted **only for manifests with <=25 direct
dependencies** (bounded because it fetches packuments per candidate version);
larger manifests fail fast with the JAGR-Infinite error. `--help` now says this.
No action needed unless you relied on a spelling selecting a distinct strategy —
none did.

### `http-reqwest` is a default feature (the earlier "no longer default" entry is superseded)

The `### Changed` entry above titled "`http-reqwest` is no longer a default
feature" describes an intermediate state and **no longer matches `Cargo.toml`**:
`default = ["runtime", "bundle", "http-reqwest"]`. No measured hot-path win is
claimed either way — the `http2_download` module is not called by default
installs. If you build with `--no-default-features`, add `http-reqwest` only when
you need the resumable-download path.

### CI and docs no longer point at deleted automation

The report generators and the standalone KPI scripts used by
`.github/workflows/benchmark.yml` and the README live under `archive/scripts/`
(they are not currently wired into `scripts/`, which keeps `benchmark.py`,
`bench/guardrails.py`, `mem_guard.py`, `guardrails.sh`, `benchmark.sh` and the
release/PGO helpers). CI invokes them from `archive/scripts/`; the README's
"Benchmarking and reports" section lists what is live and how to restore a
generator. `INNOVATION_LOG.md` lives at `docs/internal/INNOVATION_LOG.md`.
